This simplifies to $1+c = d$.  Hence, $(c,d)$ is $(1,2)$.  To check, we note that the direction vector of the line is $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}$, and its normal vector is then given as the cross product of this direction vector and the vector pointing to any point on the line:  \[\begin{pmatrix} 1+0 \\ 3(0) \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3(0) \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\]Thus the general vector pointing to any point on the line is $\begin{pmatrix} 1+0 \\ 3(0) \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + k \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1+k \\ 3k \\ 1-k \end{pmatrix}$

Then the normal vector to the plane is given by the cross product:

$\begin{pmatrix} 1+k \\ 3k \\ 1-k \end{pmatrix} \times \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3k-3 \\ -k \\ 3+3k \end{pmatrix}.$

We must have that $\begin{pmatrix} 3k - 3 \\ -k \\ 3 +3k \end{pmatrix}
= c \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} c \\ 3c \\ c \end{pmatrix}.$

Then $c = 3k - 3$, $-k = 3c$ and $3 + 3k = c$.

Clearly, $k = -1$ and so $(c,d) = \boxed{(3,0)}.$

